CAT = "cemapConduct"; SEG = "CND"
Q = [
("How many Principles for Businesses are there in the FCA Handbook?",
 "12", ["8", "10", "11"],
 "There are 12 Principles. Principle 12, the Consumer Duty, was added from 31 July 2023."),
("Principle 12, the Consumer Duty, requires a firm to:",
 "Act to deliver good outcomes", ["Maximise returns for shareholders", "Offer the cheapest product", "Remove all risk for customers"],
 "Principle 12 sets a higher standard than treating customers fairly: firms must act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers."),
("Which is one of the Consumer Duty's three cross-cutting rules?",
 "Avoid causing foreseeable harm", ["Never charge customers a fee", "Advise on every product it sells", "Guarantee investment returns"],
 "The cross-cutting rules are to act in good faith, avoid causing foreseeable harm and enable and support customers to pursue their financial objectives."),
("Which is one of the four Consumer Duty outcomes?",
 "Price and value", ["Profit and growth", "Speed of sale", "Market share"],
 "The four outcomes are products and services, price and value, consumer understanding and consumer support."),
("Under the price and value outcome, a firm must make sure:",
 "The price is fair for the benefits", ["Its price is the lowest available", "It never changes its prices", "Its prices match its competitors"],
 "Fair value means the total price is reasonable compared with the benefits the customer gets. A product need not be the cheapest to give fair value."),
("Under the consumer support outcome, cancelling a product or switching provider should be:",
 "No harder than buying it", ["Possible only by letter", "Allowed after a year only", "Handled by the FCA"],
 "Firms must not put up unreasonable barriers, sometimes called sludge practices, to cancelling, switching or complaining."),
("Which Principle requires a firm to manage conflicts of interest fairly?",
 "Principle 8", ["Principle 1", "Principle 4", "Principle 11"],
 "Principle 8 covers conflicts of interest, between the firm and customers and between customers. Principle 11 covers relations with regulators."),
("Principle 11 requires a firm to:",
 "Be open and cooperative with regulators", ["Hold enough capital at all times", "Keep client money safe and separate", "Observe proper market conduct standards"],
 "Firms must be open with the FCA and tell it about anything it would reasonably expect notice of. Capital is Principle 4 and client assets Principle 10."),
("Which Principle requires a firm to maintain adequate financial resources?",
 "Principle 4", ["Principle 2", "Principle 6", "Principle 9"],
 "Principle 4 is financial prudence. Principle 2 is skill, care and diligence, and Principle 9 concerns suitable advice where customers rely on judgement."),
("Under UK MAR, which is a form of market abuse?",
 "Spreading false rumours", ["Selling shares at a loss", "Buying shares on public news", "Holding shares for years"],
 "Market manipulation, including disseminating misleading information, is market abuse, as are insider dealing and unlawfully disclosing inside information."),
("Treating customers fairly (TCF) came before the Consumer Duty. Under the Duty, firms are expected to:",
 "Go further than TCF required", ["Stop thinking about fairness", "Apply TCF to staff only", "Replace it with sales targets"],
 "The Consumer Duty builds on TCF but is more demanding, with a focus on evidence that customers actually get good outcomes."),
("Who is responsible for a firm's culture and its approach to the Consumer Duty?",
 "The board and senior managers", ["Each individual customer", "The FCA's own supervisors", "The firm's external auditors"],
 "Culture is set from the top. Under the Duty, the board must review and approve a report on customer outcomes at least once a year."),
("Under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, an unfair term in a consumer contract is:",
 "Not binding on the consumer", ["Binding on both of the parties", "Void for the firm only", "Valid if in small print"],
 "An unfair term does not bind the consumer, although the rest of the contract may continue. The FCA can act on unfair terms in financial services contracts."),
("A firm sells a product with high fees and little benefit to a group it was never designed for. Which outcome is most clearly failed?",
 "Products and services", ["Consumer understanding", "Consumer support", "Prudential soundness"],
 "The products and services outcome requires products to be designed for the needs of an identified target market and sold to it."),
("Communications under the consumer understanding outcome should be:",
 "Clear and easy to act on", ["As long and detailed as possible", "Written in formal legal terms", "Sent only by post"],
 "Firms must test that communications are understood and give customers the information they need to make effective decisions, at the right time."),
("Which describes 'conduct risk'?",
 "Harm caused by how firms behave", ["The risk of a run on the bank", "The risk of rising inflation", "The risk of a sudden market crash"],
 "Conduct risk is the risk that firms or staff behave in ways that cause poor outcomes for customers or harm market integrity."),
("A mortgage broker pays advisers a bonus only for selling one lender's products. Which concern does this raise most directly?",
 "A conflict of interest", ["A capital shortfall", "A client money breach", "A personal data breach"],
 "Pay that rewards one product over others can bias advice. Principle 8 and the Consumer Duty require firms to manage such conflicts."),
("Under the Consumer Duty, 'retail customer' broadly means a customer who is:",
 "A consumer, not a professional", ["Any customer, of any type", "Only a high net worth individual", "A wholesale bank or insurer"],
 "The Duty protects retail customers, which broadly means consumers dealing with regulated firms, not professional or wholesale clients."),
("Principle 6 on customers' interests does not apply where:",
 "The Consumer Duty applies", ["The firm is a small one", "The advice is given free", "The client is over 65"],
 "Where the Consumer Duty applies, it replaces Principles 6 and 7, because the Duty sets the higher standard."),
("Case: Lena's firm finds that customers on its old variable rate pay far more than new customers for the same product. What does the Consumer Duty expect?",
 "Review its fair value", ["Raise new customer prices", "Keep quiet about it", "Close all the old accounts"],
 "The Duty applies to existing products too. A large price gap for the same product needs a fair value review and, where needed, action."),
]
